    title: "Reading Passage",
    level: "C1",
    text: "The coworking space has redefined how and where people work — offering a flexible alternative to traditional offices and the isolation of working from home. These shared workspaces provide desks, meeting rooms, high-speed internet, printing facilities, and communal kitchens — all accessible through daily passes, monthly memberships, or annual contracts. The concept, which gained traction in the mid-2000s, exploded after the pandemic normalised remote and hybrid working patterns. London alone has hundreds of coworking spaces, from industrial-chic warehouses in Shoreditch to polished corporate hubs in the City. The appeal lies in flexibility and community: freelancers, startups, remote employees, and small businesses share space without the commitment of a traditional lease. Hot-desking — sitting at any available desk rather than a fixed one — maximises space efficiency, while dedicated desks and private offices cater to those needing consistency. The social dimension is equally important: coworking spaces host networking events, workshops, talks, and social gatherings that combat the loneliness of solo work. Amenities vary widely — some spaces offer little more than desks and Wi-Fi; premium operators like WeWork, Huckletree, and Second Home provide barista coffee, wellness rooms, rooftop terraces, and curated member communities. The coworking model reflects a fundamental shift in work culture — from presence-based to output-based, from rigid schedules to flexible arrangements, and from employer-provided offices to individually chosen workspaces.",
